    <item>
      <title>Enlightenment 0.28 Released With Many Improvements</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Enlightenment-0.28</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Enlightenment-0.28</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:46:26 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="DESKTOP" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/desktop.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Enlightenment-0.27"&gt;Enlightenment 0.27&lt;/a&gt; released at the beginning of 2025 and since then there's been no major feature update, falling off their roughly 12 month release cadence. But out this Halloween is Enlightenment 0.28 now with the better part of two years worth of changes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Carsten Haitzler released Enlightenment 0.28 today and it comes with initial NetBSD operating system support, a NetworkManager module for wired and wireless networking management along with VPNs, and various other network integration improvements. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=enlightenment" alt="Enlightenment project screenshot" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Enlightenment 0.28 also comes with native PipeWire mixer support while still retaining support for PulseAudio. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There is also a variety of other audio-related enhancements to this Enlightenment update.Some of the other changes include new window actions, improved client side decoration handling, improved display mode sorting, improved backlight filtering, improved window siwtching, and a wide variety of fixes and code cleanups. On the low-level side is also improved pixmap handling, X connection recovery, and nested Wayland compositor shutdown. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Enlightenment 0.28 continues to boast both X11 and Wayland support. Downloads and more details on this Enlightenment 0.28 release via &lt;a href="https://www.enlightenment.org/news"&gt;Enlightenment.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Mint Developing New Apps For Terminal &amp; System Monitoring</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Mint-October-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Mint-October-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:36:11 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Linux Mint is out with their October 2026 status report to outline various initiatives they have underway in their lead-up to the Linux Mint 27 stable release happening on/around Christmas. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Mint developers have been working on bringing back hibernation support to Linux Mint after upstream Ubuntu had removed hibernation from the supported sleep modes.  Via Linux Mint's System Administration tool it will be possible to enable hibernation support for those wanting it as an alternative to suspend. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Mint's Nemo file manager added a preview pane for images as another desktop usability enhancement of Linux Mint 27. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=superlight_med" alt="Superlight app" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A brand new app being developed for Linux Mint is Superlight. The Superlight app is for system monitoring with CPU, RAM, GPU, network, and storage monitoring along with the likes of temperatures and fan speeds. Yet another system monitoring tool for the Linux desktop. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=velocity_med" alt="Velocity terminal app" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Mint is also developing Velocity as a new terminal emulator. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Lastly is Unfold as a small archive manager. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the hibernation support and new apps via the &lt;a href="https://blog.linuxmint.com/?p=5093"&gt;Linux Mint blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>New Linux 7.4 Feature For Reducing AI Noise Already Reverted Due To A "Crazy Beast"</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Drops-Taint-Bind</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Drops-Taint-Bind</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:15:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A new feature merged last week for Linux 7.4 to help reduce AI/LLM generated noise from invalid/irrelevant testing configurations is now reverted due to a "crazy beast" in the VFIO subsystem. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Greg Kroah-Hartman back in September &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Taint-Forced-Bind"&gt;crafted the TAINT_FORCED_BIND option&lt;/a&gt; to taint the running kernel if Linux's bind/unbind sysfs interface is used. This interface allows arbitrarily binding/unbinding hardware to a given driver. In the era of AI/LLM, this interface was abused by Syzbot and friends for trying to bind hardware to drivers not relevant to one another. This resulted in noise from irrelevant bug reports from these tools/bots that don't match any legitimate kernel use-case. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;So with the TAINT_FORCED_BIND, it would be clear to kernel developers in bug reports that sysfs bind/unbind was used and so helping quickly deduce it may not be a legitimate bug report. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The feature was &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-TAINT-FORCED-BIND"&gt;merged last week for Linux 7.4&lt;/a&gt; for what Greg KH characterized as a recent major fuzzing "attack" against the kernel. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Only after TAINT_FORCED_BIND was it realized VFIO code is relying on the bind/unbind behavior in legitimate practice. In &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261028-bind_taint-revert-v1-1-7c6b9cef30c9@linuxfoundation.org/"&gt;the revert patch&lt;/a&gt;, Greg described VFIO as a "crazy beast" in its usage: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Turns out that vfio is a crazy beast and people have been "relying" on the bind/unbind debugging feature as a way to handle passing through devices to guest instances.  Because of this (https://xkcd.com/1172/), let's revert the feature for now until we can figure out a more [agreeable] way to handle this such that normal workflows do not taint the kernel. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Odds are something like what Uwe proposes at: &lt;br /&gt;	https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="fe9d91889b8cd0cfc9c7cec8cfccccc7c6d099978ad08bd095929b97909bd395919b909799be9c9f8792979c8c9bd09d9193"&gt;[email&amp;#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;/ &lt;br /&gt;will be the best way forward and will be revisited after for after 7.4. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The feature was originally intended to stem the flow of unnecessary fuzzing reports of "broken" drivers where random devices were being bound to random drivers and of course things were going wrong.  The main syzbot tool is now not doing this anymore, and hopefully no one else starts it up again (but in this madness of LLMs these days, you can't be sure.)"&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;VFIO relies on bind/unbind for detaching PCIe devices from a Linux host driver and attaching to vfio-pci for use within VMs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The revert was &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=2a500a04d6f969318c05f46aedb6e73fc1963a00"&gt;merged&lt;/a&gt; on Friday night. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Uwe Kleine-König's proposal is for using TAINT_DRIVER_OVERRIDE just in the usage of driver_override and disabling driver_override by default while allowing Linux driver the option of allowing themselves into it. We'll see if that ends up being adopted for Linux v7.5 but is now too late for v7.4 with the merge window expected to close tomorrow.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.9 Lands Some New Performance Optimizations</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.9-More-Performance</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.9-More-Performance</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:51:01 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; I love a good performance optimization and there are several this week in the KDE space with Plasma 6.9 development heating up. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This Week in Plasma is out with its latest issue to highlight the newest KDE changes as we close out the month of October. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those using KWin's zoom effect, with Plasma 6.9 the zoom effect will have a much lower performance impact. The zoom plugin will now skip processing low-contrast pixels like for flat and solid areas, which is leading to significant speed-ups: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=plasma_69_more_perf" alt="Plasma zoom speed-up" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.9 is also now clearing stale entries for desktop file and folder positions in its configuration file, to avoid it growing massively over time and slowing down Plasma overall. This stems from a &lt;a href="https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=469445"&gt;2023 bug report&lt;/a&gt; over the screen mapping entry in the config file growing infinitely enough that it slows down the Plasma shell and could crash it. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With Plasma 6.9, the Discover software center will now allow copying and sharing of &lt;em&gt;appstream://&lt;/em&gt;URLs for locally-installed Flatpak packages. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Meanwhile this week's &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8.2"&gt;Plasma 6.8.2 release&lt;/a&gt; brought some crash fixes and also addressed other regressions. With Plasma 6.8.2 it also included a regression that could degrade gaming performance due in KWin, which is fixed by disabling post-blend offloading if not supported by the DRM color pipelines. Plasma 6.8.3 will bring some additional crash/regression fixes in November. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also exciting this week on the fixes front is KDE Frameworks now &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Fixes-15-Year-Old-Bugs"&gt;fixing a 15 year old bug over file completion to USB storage devices&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these changes via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/10/31/this-week-in-plasma-an-avalanche-of-performance-improvements-and-bug-fixes/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ceph In Linux 7.4 Adds Global Mount Option For Lazy I/O</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Ceph</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Ceph</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:30:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Ceph kernel client changes have been submitted for Linux 7.4 ahead of its merge window expected to wrap up on Sunday. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Ceph has supported an ioctl for enabling lazy I/O on a per-file basis while the new code for Linux 7.4 is adding the "lazyio" mount option if wanting to enable lazy I/O globally. Ceph's lazy I/O mode will enable it for all regular files at open time. The mode allows for buffered I/O via the page cache, even if multiple clients have the file open for write. That scenario is intended for HPC workloads and similar that can cope with relaxed cache coherency. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=ceph_lazy" alt="Ceph lazyio" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In addition to the "lazyio" mount option for Ceph, the inine data feature that has been around for more than a decade as an experimental feature and deprecated since 2020 is in the process of being removed. For Linux 7.4 the kernel adds a &lt;em&gt;CEPH_FS_INLINE_DATA&lt;/em&gt; Kconfig option that defaults to off. So those still relying on Ceph inline data support will now need to ensure that CEPH_FS_INLINE_DATA=Y is set to continue using it. But in a future release that plan to remove the deprecated code outright. Ceoh inline data support was &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/MTg2Njg"&gt;added in Linux 3.19 back in 2014&lt;/a&gt; for allowing small files to be stored directly inside the file's inode or metadata data structures rather than separate RADOS objects. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261030181734.3806816-1-idryomov@gmail.com/"&gt;Ceph pull request&lt;/a&gt; also brings a number of bug fixes and other code clean-ups for Linux 7.4.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GNOME Now Has An Established RFC Process, GNOME OS Continues Improving</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Establishes-RFC</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Establishes-RFC</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:10:19 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In recent weeks &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-RFC-Process-Discussion"&gt;GNOME developers have been discussing a formal Request For Comments "RFC" process&lt;/a&gt; for solving major technical questions and changes to GNOME. This formalized process for succeeding their ad-hoc processes up to now has reached consensus. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GNOME stakeholders have reached a consensus on this RFC process led by Sophie Herold. This will help formalize and ease the discussions moving forward for major project questions/changes. Those wanting to view GNOME's RFC process can read it in full via &lt;a href="https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/rfcs/-/blob/main/content/0001-rfc-process.md?ref_type=heads"&gt;the GNOME RFCs repo&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This week the GNOME project also saw enhancements to its Nautilus "Files" file manager, GNOME Software is now more efficient with its icon handling and loading of remote assets, and GNOME Calendar also continues to be improved upon. Lower down the stack, GLib now has support for XDG Intents for letting applications register themselves in supporting opening commands in a terminal and handling certain URI schemes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GNOME OS meanwhile now enables the LDAC codec for their PipeWire builds, Zswap fixes have been merged, the extra-codecs feature is now enabled by default, and there have been NVIDIA improvements to make NVIDIA graphics work with Snap applications and adapting their firmware for the NVIDIA R615 driver. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these latest GNOME and GNOME OS enhancements via &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/10/twig-272/#gnome-fellowship"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>DXVK-NVAPI 0.9.3 Brings Nice Improvements For Linux Gamers On Latest NVIDIA Drivers</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/DXVK-NVAPI-0.9.3</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/DXVK-NVAPI-0.9.3</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX GAMING" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxgaming.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; DXVK-NVAPI 0.9.3 is out this weekend as a new feature update to this NVIDIA NVAPI implementation used by Valve as part of Steam Play with Proton for nicely integrating NVIDIA technologies into various Windows games running on Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With DXVK-NVAPI 0.9.3 is now support for Opacity Micromaps "OMM" as part of Microsoft's DirectX Raytracing (DXR 1.2+) via NVAPI. With the NVIDIA R615 Linux driver or newer and VKD3D-Proton 3.1, this allows leveraging the Opacity Micromaps feature of DXR for speeding up ray-tracing through alpha-tested and semi-transparent geometry. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=omm" alt="OMM" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about Opacity Micromaps with DXR can learn more via &lt;a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/directx/omm/"&gt;this Microsoft dev blog&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;DXVK-NVAPI 0.9.3 also brings an alternative NVIDIA Reflex implementation for Vulkan games using the VK_NV_low_latency revision 2 extension. This also benefits those on the NVIDIA R615 driver or newer and using this implementation makes the DXVK-NVAPI Vulkan layer obsolete. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The updated DXVK-NVAPI also supports new driver settings found in NVIDIA R610 and R615 drivers, improves reporting of available/free VRAM, and supports compiling for ARM64. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on DXVK-NVAPI 0.9.3 via &lt;a href="https://github.com/jp7677/dxvk-nvapi/releases/tag/v0.9.3"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>CUDA Oxide 0.3 Vastly Improves Rust Support For NVIDIA CUDA Programming</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/CUDA-Oxide-0.3</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/CUDA-Oxide-0.3</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:50:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="NVIDIA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/nvidia.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; NVIDIA software engineers released CUDA Oxide 0.3 for closing out October. CUDA Oxide as a reminder is their &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/NVIDIA-CUDA-Oxide-0.1"&gt;experimental Rust to CUDA compiler&lt;/a&gt; that has been seeing a lot of activity in recent months. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With CUDA Oxide 0.3, cuda-oxide is now using the official stable Rust version, currently Rust 1.99.1. In prior versions of cuda-oxide, running Rust nightly builds were required. The new release also unifies the host runtime with cutile-rs in the cuda-rust monorepo. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Another big step forward for cuda-oxide 0.3 is shipping an experimental version of "cute-rs" as the CUTLASS CuTe in Rust. With cute-rs it's able to run end-to-end Qwen3-8B at BF16 + NVFP4 along with Qwen3-TTS. Those models with cute-rs are being tested on NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For improving the debugging experience, v0.3 also has full cuda-gdb DWARF debugging support. Plus there is now in-kernel event tracing, register and spill diagnostics, and improved testing. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The new release also brings performance and compiler optimizations for Blackwell and Hopper GPUs. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=cuda_oxide_03" alt="CUDA Oxide 0.3" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details for CUDA Oxide 0.3 via &lt;a href="https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-rust/releases/tag/cuda-oxide/v0.3.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Sculpt OS 26.10 Released - Now Powered By Genode's Custom Microkernel</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Sculpt-OS-26.10</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Sculpt-OS-26.10</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:37:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Sculpt+OS"&gt;Sculpt OS&lt;/a&gt; as the general purpose operating system project born out of the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Genode"&gt;Genode&lt;/a&gt; initiative is now using the project's own microkernel rather than a third-party microkernel. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sculpt OS 26.10 debuted on Friday and notable is now using the custom Genode microkernel: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Sculpt 26.10 is the first version based on Genode's custom microkernel instead of relying on a 3rd-party kernel. This kernel - designed from the ground up for the needs of Genode and Sculpt OS in particular - is not only more "micro" than any other of the microkernels supported by Genode, it also solves long-standing deficiencies concerning CPU scheduling and kernel-memory management. Sculpt OS finally stands on Genode's own footing!"&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sculpt OS 26.10 also now includes VirtualBox 7 components for running in VMs rather than VirtualBox 6. Additionally this OS update brings better web camera handling, new support for UK keyboards, and other improvements. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=sculptos_2610" alt="Sculpt OS 26.10" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on Sculpt OS 26.10 via &lt;a href="https://genode.org/news/sculpt-os-release-26.10"&gt;Genode.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.21 Released With Better WoW64 OpenGL Performance</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.21-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.21-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:29:01 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Wine 11.21 is out as the newest bi-weekly development release of Wine and as we near Wine 12.0 stable release happenings getting underway in about one month with the official v12.0 release expected early in the new year. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Most exciting with Wine 11.21 is having merged &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-Mesa-Map-Buffer-Client-Ptr"&gt;support for the MESA_map_buffer_client_pointer OpenGL extension&lt;/a&gt; to improve WoW64 32-bit OpenGL. This should end up improving the performance for those using OpenGL with a WoW64 setup. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.21 also brings an initial version of the Voice Capture DSP library. Rounding out the release is improved support for user accounts. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.21 also has 20 known bug fixes, including for Shift + Ctrl + Tab not working, issues with Cinebench 2026, and game fixes like for Skyrim SE and World of Tanks. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of changes with Wine 11.21 can be found at &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.21"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
